# Model ranking — spec 0.3 + hotfix vlp.1 (exclusion + capability tiers),
# sort order fixed by capability-class bug `en9` (see CHANGELOG): the
# ranking-file position below is the PRIMARY sort key, not a tie-break of
# last resort — that was the pre-en9 (and pre-fkm.9) behavior this file
# used to describe.
#
# This file is an EXCLUSION list and a CAPABILITY-CLASS order. It does
# NOT restrict which models the resolver considers — every candidate not
# excluded below is still eligible, including families this file has
# never heard of. Unknown families are FIRST-CLASS candidates: they are
# ranked in the TOP capability class, so a newly-launched tier above
# Fable still wins automatically (by recency, WITHIN that top class)
# without an edit to this file. Day-zero adoption survives en9 unchanged
# — only the mechanism producing it changed; see "Resolver sort" below.
#
# Syntax
# ------
# Lines starting with `!` are exclusions:
#
#     !claude-haiku       # drop every claude-haiku-* model
#
# Lines without `!` are family prefixes, listed BEST TIER FIRST. A line's
# position here is that family's CAPABILITY CLASS — the resolver's
# PRIMARY sort key (see "Resolver sort" below), not a fallback used only
# when two candidates tie on created_at and max_input_tokens. A known
# lower-tier family never outranks a known higher-tier family, however
# much newer it is.
#
# Comments (`#`) and blank lines are ignored. Whitespace is trimmed.
#
# Resolver sort (primary -> tertiary) — en9
# -------------------------------------------
#   1. capability class ASC — this file's family-prefix position (unknown
#      family = TOP class). This is now the PRIMARY key: with recency
#      primary (the pre-en9 behavior), a newer-but-lesser family could
#      hijack `top` and collapse the role split — reproduced with
#      claude-opus-5 (2026-07-24) displacing claude-fable-5 (2026-06-07).
#   2. created_at DESC — newest wins WITHIN a class (ISO 8601 from
#      /v1/models).
#   3. max_input_tokens DESC — larger context wins a same-class date tie.
#
# When the resolver picks a winner whose created_at is missing or
# unparseable, the SessionStart notice prints LOUDLY with the manual
# `/workflow-model <id>` adopt command and refuses to auto-rewrite the
# pins — so a malformed listing can never silently switch the workflow.
#
# Why this is set up as exclusion-only
# ------------------------------------
# Before hotfix vlp.1 this file was a strict allow-list: only families
# whose prefix appeared here could be selected, with version-tuple
# sorting inside each family. That meant a brand-new family tier shipped
# upstream (e.g. above Fable) would be invisible to the resolver until
# someone hand-edited this file. Reversing the polarity — exclusion plus
# a capability-tier order, with an unrecognised family ranked in the top
# class — fixes principle 1 ("auto-upgrade") without requiring this file
# to track upstream releases. (The sort's own primary key later moved
# from recency to capability class under `en9` — day-zero adoption for a
# genuinely new top-tier family is unaffected: it still wins on recency,
# now within the top class rather than file-wide.)
#
# Tier list (capability-class order, best to worst — the resolver's
# PRIMARY sort key since en9, not a tie-break for same-date candidates):

